sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-16> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "There is no simple and constant relation, holding throughout life, between the size of the whole body and that of organs such as brain, heart and liver; the brain in particular falls from about one-eighth of body weight at birth to about one-fiftieth at twenty-five.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Organ size bears no constant ratio to body size through life, especially the brain";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter III, “The Rate of Growth”; topic “Rate of growth of organs”. Scope: humans and other vertebrates (lion, dogfish)"
}
